 ## Background
-The use of wavelet transforms for volume rendering compression emerged in the early 2000s as a response to the memory bandwidth limitations of GPUs. Key early works include those by Guthe et al. [S2, unofficial], who demonstrated hierarchical wavelet representations allowing compression ratios of 30:1, and Wetekam et al. [S1], who proposed octree-based multiresolution approaches. The field has since diversified into specialized variants like Laplacian pyramids for on-the-fly rendering [S1][S2, unofficial][S3], biorthogonal filters for phase preservation [S2, unofficial], and video-coding-inspired slice compression [S1]. Recent developments include the application of these transforms to neural field representations via mask compression [S1].
+The use of wavelets for volume rendering compression emerged in the early 2000s as a means to handle large medical datasets on GPUs. Wetekam et al. [WSKW05] proposed a multiresolution approach using an octree to store wavelet transforms, decoded via FPGA hardware [S1]. Wu et al. [WQ05] introduced a modified 3-D dyadic wavelet transform with Rice coding for medical images [S1]. Laplacian pyramids were adapted for volume rendering by Guthe et al. [GY95], using low-pass filtering and delta encoding to allow on-the-fly reconstruction [S1]. Muraki [12] presented a 3D orthogonal wavelet approximation using tensor products of 1D bases [S2, unofficial]. Guthe et al. [7] demonstrated hierarchical wavelet representations allowing 30:1 compression without noticeable artifacts [S2, unofficial]. More recent work includes Shearlets for directional features [S1] and Mask Compression Pipelines for neural field representations [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
